# The runner's libc was newer than the image's, so both Docker labs were measuring the runner (2026-10-06)

`scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh` and `scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh` are the two
acceptance labs that compile this tree and then interrogate the resulting program inside a
container that has never seen the repository. Until today they compiled it **on the host that
started them** and copied the binary in. Scheduled run 37297270032 went red because of that,
and the more uncomfortable fact is that the labs had been green for weeks on the one machine
whose libc is older than the image's: a verdict about a container was really a verdict about
whoever happened to type the command.

This batch compiles inside the image that runs the artifact, refuses to deploy bytes it cannot
tie to that build, and stops the TLS lab from printing a log file that cannot contain the
failure it is reporting.

== 0. What is in this batch

    scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh       cargo build inside ${IMAGE}; sha256 guard on the
                                              deployed bytes; readiness failure now dumps the
                                              backend's own log instead of `docker logs`
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh    same build inside ${IMAGE} for `-p chaos-engine
                                              --bins`, five deploy sites now copy from
                                              ${lab_root}/artifacts and each is digest-checked
    .github/workflows/docker-labs.yml         drops the dotslash, protoc, rustup/toolchain and
                                              rust-cache steps: nothing compiles on the runner
                                              any more, so the runner needs no toolchain

== 1. The scheduled run, verbatim

    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:39.0176006Z    the container runs this binary: 20M
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:39.0176809Z
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:39.0177208Z == issuing a lab CA and a certificate for chaos.test
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:39.6375506Z    chain verifies: Oct  7 10:38:39 2026 GMT
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:39.6403989Z
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:38:40.5199787Z    backend address on the shared network: 172.18.0.2:8787
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:39:17.4907102Z the backend never answered on loopback inside its container; giving up.
    web TLS deployment and M4 remote acceptance  2026-10-05T10:39:17.8604184Z ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

Thirty-seven seconds of readiness polling, then a sentence that names no cause. The line above
the failure, `the container runs this binary: 20M`, is the old script announcing the size of the
binary it had just built on the runner and copied in. It printed a size and no digest, so the
run cannot even be asked which bytes were deployed.

The remote lab never got to run: both labs are steps in the same job and the TLS lab exits 1.

== 2. Why it was green on the machine that wrote it

Measured today on the three systems involved:

    host (Ubuntu 22.04, where the labs have been run by hand)   glibc 2.35
    ubuntu:24.04 (what actions' ubuntu-latest image is)         glibc 2.39
    chaos-verify:local (docker/verify.Dockerfile, bookworm)     glibc 2.36

and the highest libc symbol version the host-built binary actually asks for:

    objdump -T target/debug/chaos-web | grep -o 'GLIBC_[0-9.]*' | sort -uV | tail -1
        GLIBC_2.34

2.34 is satisfiable by 2.36, so the mounted binary loads in the image when it was built on this
host, on any box at 2.35 or 2.36, and fails to load when it was built on 2.39. That is the whole
mechanism: the labs were green on every machine that ran them by hand and red only on the runner,
and nothing in the lab's own output could distinguish "the deployed program is broken" from "the
deployed program was built for a newer libc than this container has".

== 3. The proof, run today

A binary from the runner's userspace, executed inside the image the labs deploy into:

    docker run --rm -v "$PWD/glibc:/x" ubuntu:24.04 cp /bin/ls /x/ls-2404
    docker run --rm -v "$PWD/glibc:/x:ro" chaos-verify:local /x/ls-2404 /x

    /x/ls-2404: /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_2.38' not found (required by /x/ls-2404)
    rc=1

`/bin/ls` is not a lab artifact and no Rust code is involved. Anything the 24.04 runner compiled
hit the same wall, which is what `chaos-web` did in section 1.

== 4. The fix: compile where the artifact runs, and prove the bytes came from there

Both labs now run their build as a `docker run` of `${IMAGE}` over the repository, writing the
binaries into the lab directory together with their sha256, and every deploy site copies from
that directory and is checked against it before anything is launched:

    chaos-web: the deployed artifact is ${got:0:12}… but ${IMAGE} built ${want:0:12}…

The digest guard is not decoration for the build step. Without it, replacing the copy source with
anything else -- a stale path, a host build, an earlier run's leftovers in the lab root -- leaves
a lab that still prints 41 or 130 `ok` lines about a program nobody built in this run. Section 6
measures that.

The build reuses the same named volumes `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` already warms
(`chaos-verify-cargo-registry`, `chaos-verify-cargo-git`, `chaos-verify-target`), so a developer
box does not pay for two dependency graphs. On the runner those volumes start empty, which is why
the workflow still passes `CARGO_ARGS: "--locked"` rather than the labs' local default of
`--offline --locked`.

Because nothing compiles on the runner now, the workflow's dotslash, protoc, rustup toolchain and
`Swatinem/rust-cache` steps were removed along with the apt packages that fed them. The runner
needs `ca-certificates`, `git`, `socat` (the remote lab's tunnel) and `openssl` (the lab CA).

== 5. The second defect: the lab printed a log that could not contain the failure

The readiness branch used to end in `docker logs --tail 20 "${web_container}"`. That container is
started with `sleep infinity` as its PID 1, and the backend is launched into it with
`docker exec -d`, redirecting to `/work/backend.log`. So `docker logs` of that container is the
output of `sleep`, always empty. The one sentence CI had to go on was the only sentence it could
not have improved, because the evidence of the load failure sat in a file the script never read.

The branch now dumps the file it did write:

    the backend never answered on loopback inside its container; giving up.
    --- /work/backend.log ---
    /lab/bin/chaos-web: /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_2.38' not found (required by /lab/bin/chaos-web)
    --- end of /work/backend.log ---

That transcript is not from the runner. It is W2 below: the old behaviour, reproduced on this
machine by deploying a 24.04 binary with the digest guard made vacuous.

== 6. Mutations

Three single-anchor edits, each applied to the working tree, run, and reverted; the driver prints
`restore byte-identical=True` and it held for all three. Baselines before each run were the green
labs of section 7, and the driver refuses to start on a red baseline.

W1 -- web lab, the deployed artifact pointed back at the host build. Caught:

    == building the Web host inside chaos-verify:local
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 0.50s
    chaos-web: the deployed artifact is a05422e83788… but chaos-verify:local built 0e5d40adb4f4…
    ### lab rc = 1

W2 -- web lab, deploy the `ubuntu:24.04` `/bin/ls` with the digest guard made vacuous, i.e. the
pre-fix situation as the runner saw it. Caught, and now self-explaining:

    == building the Web host inside chaos-verify:local
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 0.51s
       the container runs this binary: 140K (sha256 2726eb358646…, built by chaos-verify:local)
    ...
       backend address on the shared network: 172.19.0.2:8787
    the backend never answered on loopback inside its container; giving up.
    --- /work/backend.log ---
    /lab/bin/chaos-web: /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6: version `GLIBC_2.38' not found (required by /lab/bin/chaos-web)
    --- end of /work/backend.log ---
    ### lab rc = 1

    Note the asymmetry: the header line still says `built by chaos-verify:local`, because W2 also
    edited the line that produces that claim. The readiness dump is the half of the fix that does
    not depend on the guard being intact, which is the reason both halves went in.

R1 -- remote lab, the CLI deployed into the dev container replaced with the host's `/bin/ls`.
Caught, before any container was launched:

    == building the artifacts inside chaos-verify:local
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 0.41s
    chaos-remote: /tmp/chaos-remote-lab.mbDTUw/run-1829647/dev/bin/chaos-remote is f32928d24284… but chaos-verify:local built 3600c1c9bba2…
    ### lab rc = 1

== 7. Both labs after the fix, on this machine

The web lab built in-image and passed every check:

    == building the Web host inside chaos-verify:local
       the container runs this binary: 20M (sha256 0e5d40adb4f4…, built by chaos-verify:local)
    ...
    all 41 TLS deployment checks passed against nginx:1.27-alpine + chaos-verify:local

The remote lab likewise, across its five topologies (host, dev, disk-full, noexec, and the
tunnel-dropping pair):

    == building the artifacts inside chaos-verify:local
    ...
       ok  a WebSocket handshake completes through the forward
       ok  and the port is gone once the session that used it is over

    == summary
    all 130 M4 acceptance checks passed against chaos-verify:local

Warm-cache rebuilds inside the image finish in half a second (the 0.41s/0.50s/0.51s lines above),
so compiling where the artifact runs costs a developer nothing on a warm tree; only the first run
on a cold runner pays, and that is the run whose verdict is the point.

== 8. What this still does not prove

- The green runs above are on a bookworm image on a 22.04 host. The fix removes the host from the
  question, but the runner leg itself is only re-proven once a scheduled or dispatched run of
  `Docker acceptance labs` goes green on `ubuntu-latest`; that verdict is appended to this file,
  not assumed here.
- `chaos-verify:local` is Debian bookworm. The release artifacts shipped to users are built
  elsewhere; nothing in this file is evidence about the libc floor of a shipped binary. That floor
  is a release question this batch does not open.
- The remote lab still runs both its machines from one daemon on one host. It has always been a
  single-host lab with two containers, and the ledger row for it in `scripts/ci/docker-labs.tsv`
  says so.
